  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 1.* $v = 60\/3.0 = 20$ m s⁻¹ and $f = 1\/0.050 = 20$ Hz, so $lambda = 20\/20 = 1.0$ m.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 2.* $f' = 600 times 340\/(340 + 30) = 551$ Hz. A receding source always lowers the note.
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 3.* $1\/v = -1\/15 - 1\/20 = -7\/60$, so $v = -8.6$ cm: virtual, behind the mirror.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 4.* $sin c = 1\/1.33 = 0.752$, so $c = 49°$ — larger than the 42° of glass, because water is less refractive.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 5.* $1\/v = 1\/10 - 1\/5 = -1\/10$, so $v = -10$ cm: virtual, on the same side, twice the size.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 6.* Gamma rays have the shortest wavelengths and therefore the highest frequencies.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 1.* Four loops fit on the string, so $4 lambda\/2 = 1.2$ m and $lambda = 0.60$ m.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 2.* $lambda\/2 = 51 - 17 = 34$ cm, so $lambda = 0.68$ m and $v = 500 times 0.68 = 340$ m s⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 3.* The ray travels along the normal, so $i = 0$ and $r = 0$: it returns along its own path.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 4.* $v = c\/n = (3.0 times 10^8)\/2.42 = 1.2 times 10^8$ m s⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 5.* The cornea and lens form a real image on the retina, and a real image from a converging system is inverted.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 6.* Cyan, magenta and yellow are the subtractive primaries used with pigments and printing inks.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 1.* $v = sqrt(T\/mu)$, so doubling the speed needs four times the tension — the square root is the trap.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 2.* Quality is the number and strength of the overtones present above the fundamental.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 3.* $1\/v = 1\/15 - 1\/10 = -1\/30$, so $v = -30$ cm: virtual and magnified three times.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 4.* Apparent depth $= 9.0\/1.5 = 6.0$ cm, so the shift is $9.0 - 6.0 = 3.0$ cm.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 5.* $P = 5.0 - 2.0 = +3.0$ D, so $f = 1\/3.0 = 0.33$ m $= 33$ cm.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 6.* Sound is a mechanical compression wave; the other three are electromagnetic.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 1.* A node and the next antinode are a quarter of a wavelength apart.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 2.* About 340 m s⁻¹. The figure 1500 is the speed in water and $3.0 times 10^8$ is the speed of light.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 3.* Beyond the focus the rays converge and form a real image; inside the focus the image is virtual.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 4.* $sin i_2 = 1.5 sin 40° = 0.964$, so $i_2 = 75°$ — the ray escapes, because 40° is below the critical angle of 42°.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 5.* The near point is 25 cm; the far point is at infinity for a normal eye.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 6.* $E = 100\/5.0^2 = 4.0$ lux.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 1.* $v = 2500 times 0.136 = 340$ m s⁻¹. The wavelength must be converted to metres first.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 2.* The open pipe sounds at $v\/2L$ and the closed one at $v\/4L$, so the ratio is $2:1$.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 3.* The image is the same size as the object and the same distance behind the mirror, so $m = 1$.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 4.* $d = i_1 + i_2 - A$, so $i_2 = d + A - i_1 = 40 + 60 - 45 = 55°$.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 5.* The eyepiece magnifies the image formed by the objective, so the two magnifications multiply.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 6.* A gap comparable with the wavelength spreads the wave appreciably; a wide gap barely spreads it at all.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 1.* Three crests a second is $f = 3$ Hz and the crest spacing is the wavelength, so $v = 3 times 5.0 = 15$ m s⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 2.* $f' = 500 times 340\/(340 + 40) = 447$ Hz.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 3.* With the object between F and C the image is beyond C, real, inverted and magnified — the projector arrangement.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 4.* Both conditions are needed: dense-to-rare direction and an angle greater than the critical angle.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 5.* The camera lens forms a real, inverted image on the film or sensor, as the eye does on the retina.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 6.* Only a transverse wave can have its vibrations restricted to one plane, so polarisation proves light is transverse.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 1.* $f = 1\/0.020 = 50$ Hz, so $v = 50 times 4.0 = 200$ m s⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 2.* $d = v t \/ 2 = 1500 times 0.40 \/ 2 = 300$ m.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 3.* A mirror of large aperture gathers more light and suffers no chromatic aberration, since it does not refract at all.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock2"))[
    *Chapter 6.* Red is deviated least of all, so it lies nearest the undeviated direction of the incident beam.
  ]
  ]
]
